eed help  they do have some idea that robots could  help them  but they don't know really how they  don't know how to what type of robots  how to access them  and um  often it's also not all that affordable  at least they don't think that these  robots are affordable and then we uh  start asking questions and these are the  questions that very int very much  interest myself and my students  questions about policy  how could governments step in and help  the different facilities uh bring in the  robots incorporate them into the work  done in the facilities  especially under these extreme  conditions of a pandemic  so following up on that last point um  i  i guess there's there's a question that  again could be for both of you uh in  terms of what are the barriers to  deploying robots both on the on the  technical side but also on the political  side  yeah so from the technical side i mean  there's still things to be done uh if  you want to deploy robots to be able to  be autonomous in these kind of  environments you're looking at you know  safe navigation through hallways and in  residence rooms and so on so  human-centered environments robots  knowing things from social skills like  how do you navigate when there's other  people in the hallway you know passing  um on the standing on the right letting  people pass you on the left and so on  things things that you know we take for  granted when we're when we're navigating  ourselves or walking through hallways  and then there's of course um a  challenge with older adults  we're promoting  natural communication with these robots  so social assistive robots provide  assistance in a non-contact way so they  they interact with people the same way  we interact with each other so through  you know verbal communication or  non-verbal communication and so  speech recognition becomes a huge  element of these robots and uh it's  challenging uh looking at different user  groups and trying to understand their  speech so for robot