wer when they  decide to move in into this new policy  area  somewhat of a follow-up but maybe uh  taking us in a bit of a different  direction  amish i think this question is for you  um you know i can imagine a world in  which some people would be thrilled by  the vision of robots taking care of the  elderly for example but others i suspect  might find that um a frightening  prospect do you think that uh patients  nursing home residents etc uh would be  willing to accept health care services  administered by a robot and how do we  how do we in canada compare this in  respect to other publics across the  world in israel for example from your  studies um i suspect that goldie uh  probably knows more about this actually  than i do but i'll give it a stat  nonetheless  now  what i would argue is that we already  know something for quite some time uh in  the world of research about technology  acceptance  and what shapes technology acceptance  actually it goes back to uh different  studies i think it was davis who  initiated this uh area of research back  in the end of the 1980s  and he formulated of a technology  acceptance model why do people decide  that the technology is for them or not  uh one reason is because of the  technology is perceived as useful  is it a useful technology or not  and from our interviews uh with uh  different stakeholders in the system it  seems that most of them are quite  convinced now that robotics certainly  has a whole lot of potential to help  them a lot so at least from the  perspective  this is not the patients i know but the  facility managers  uh certainly robots are something that  they would like to have uh getting them  into the facilities  as goldie can attest is a whole  different matter that's more complicated  but at least they would like to have  uh these uh robots because they think  they might be useful  um it might also be true of many of the  residents patients and the health care  system that robots could help them  and we also know uh from research that  one of the most important  factors  that shape people's preferences in terms  of technology in general and robotics  specifically  um is that they are to some extent  already familiar with similar  technologies so if they already have  experience with similar technologies the  likelihood that they would be uh  accepting of new technologies increases  um now this of course uh leads to the  question  if we're not if we're talking for  example about the elderly  that as we know uh  quite often have less  past experience with technologies than  the younger generations  what happens then  uh then there are other factors that  matter quite a lot there's about ease of  use how easy is it to use the technology  and the good news is that uh robotic  developers like goldie here  know that this is a vital competent  of robotics  robotics doesn't help if you can't use  it  and so it's really important that uh  robotics would be accessible  and i'll end with uh  international comparison  um  is canada more accepting less exp  accepting to be in other places i really  can't say but from a few studies that  have actually systematically compared uh  acceptance of new technologies and  robots across  cross countries what uh the researchers  have found but cultural differences  actually are less important than we  might think but everybody thinks that in  japan people you know are robotics  crazed they love robots and all that but  it seems that um if you ask them the  same questions the differences between  americans and the japanese are not that  great  uh it many times really boils down to do  they have past experience with similar  technologies  uh is it going to be useful  and uh is it easy to use uh goldie  sure yeah so we've been doing research  in this for now close to 20 years so  we've we've deployed our robots and  long-term care facilities health care  facilities you name it and we've done  hundreds upon hundreds of human robot  inter