ell so  one-on-one interactions  uh with residents with patients it frees  up time for doing that and we all know  how important that is so certainly uh  robots  could help be already due and hopefully  uh this is going to expand  yeah maybe i can just add to that robots  are great at doing the 3ds dirty dull  and dangerous jobs so if you think about  that um you know as amos was saying  there's a lot of repetitive work  um for caregivers to do and robots can  take care of those repetitive jobs they  can do it with a smile on their face you  know so they can so for example  recreational activities that sometimes  you know when when there's a lot of  tasks for caregivers to do those are put  kind of down on the priority list and so  there's not as much engagement with the  the residents and cognitive engagement  as we all know and social engagement is  really important to residents living in  long-term care homes especially you know  for overall moods  minimizing depression as well as helping  with dementia right interventions are  really important with that so if robots  could facilitate you know activities  memory activities whatever it may be  um with the group and they can do that  you know throughout the course of a day  rather than just having it one time  a day for you know whatever number of  days but they could do it every day for  a certain number of hours it really  would help with you know on the on the  resident's side with their overall  well-being and health as well as the  staff side because you know they're not  jumping from activity to activity so  they can focus on the higher skill tasks  because we can't say robots are going to  take over all the tasks that humans do  um that's not in close to our future  right now the idea is that they can  supplement and support the caregivers  and it helps reduce the stress and  workload they have especially during the  pandemic right so a lot of activities  have been cut out you know external  people that used